- action(n7, "Request exception again"): Hallucinates a rejection/retry path not documented in the source ("After the exception is approved, the next step is course enrollment"), and lacks outgoing precedence, creating a dead end.
- action(n18, "Merge accreditation and exam date announcement"): Hallucinates a merge step; the source only specifies simultaneous execution before moving forward ("At the same time, two additional steps should be completed: granting accreditation and announcing the date of the exam. After signing up for the exam...").
- action(n14, "Start homework assignment") & missing precedence from n14: Breaks the intended iteration; the source dictates a cycle if homework remains ("If it was not the last homework, another homework assignment will be given"), but the model leaves this node disconnected.
- action(n24, "Sign up for exam again") → action(n25, "Return to exam attendance") → action(n27, "End"): Incorrectly terminates the process on failure; the source implies a recurring loop for retakes ("if the exam is failed, another sign-up for the exam is required"), not a final endpoint.
- defines(v1, ..., {}), defines(v3, ..., {}), defines(v4, ..., {}): Specify empty value ranges for decision variables that govern explicit conditional transitions in the source, violating faithful representation of their logical domains.
- defines(v2, "Is the exception approved", {"Continue"}) & guard(b2, c1, v2 = 'Continue'): Assigns an inconsistent value ('Continue') to an approval status variable, contradicting the source condition ("After the exception is approved").
- action(n2, "Is an exception needed..."), action(n5, "Is the exception approved"), action(n12, "Is this the last homework"), action(n22, "Did the exam pass"): Payloads are framed as interrogative decision gates rather than executable process steps, misaligning with the action element definition and the source’s chronological event descriptions.

